  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 447 AM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nLooking at the big picture, broad cyclonic flow is noted across the \nMidwest into the Northeast thanks to an upper low near the Hudson \nBay. The upper ridge dominates the Desert Southwest, placing us in \nan area of quasi-zonal flow aloft. The sfc low and associated cold \nfront have pushed south of the area with WAA and the LLJ helping to \nmaintain storms across the center of the CWA through the night, \nwhich have been very slowly making their way southward. These have \nbeen sub-severe for the last few hours and should remain so through \nthe morning. With the moisture axis overhead, some brief heavy \ndownpours are possible especially with any updrafts that quickly \npulse up and down. The current batch of thunderstorms should exit \nthe area by sunrise. Another area of sub-severe storms is making its \nway across KS with a convectively induced vort max. This is progged \nto enter north central KS around sunrise and eventually exit the \narea by late morning, likely weakening as it does so.\n\nAfter the morning rain moves out, a break is expected with most \nareas staying dry the rest of the day. Cooler temperatures are \nforecast behind the cold front with highs in the 80s. The main \nchallenge for today is whether or not another round of storms makes \nit into northern KS late this afternoon, and if so, how far south. A \nweak perturbation within the northwest flow aloft in Nebraska is \nprogged to spark convection there late this morning, which lasts \ninto the afternoon. CAMs are varied in how far south this activity \nmaintains itself, with the few more recent HRRR runs bringing \nisolated storms as far south as I-70. Most other guidance suggests \nthese storms struggle to do much as they approach our area. Forecast \nsoundings show an uncapped environment by late afternoon with 2000-\n2500 J/kg of CAPE and around 45 kts of shear, so if the storms can \nreach northern KS, the atmosphere appears more favorable to sustain \nsevere weather. Damaging wind would be the main threat with perhaps \nsome small hail.\n\nAny other convection later tonight into Thursday morning is favored \nto stay well west of the area. Surface high pressure during the day \nThursday should allow for mostly sunny skies with temperatures once \nagain reaching the 80s.\n\nFriday through the weekend, we transition to more northwesterly flow \naloft as the upper ridge to our west amplifies, as does the \ntroughing pattern to our east. Multiple embedded perturbations \nduring this time frame bring on and off chances for showers and \nthunderstorms Friday through Sunday, although the low predictability \nof these waves keeps PoPs low for any given time frame (30% or \nlower). With rain chances generally looking to be nocturnal, daytime \ntemperatures look to gradually warm a bit through the end of the \nweek with highs a few degrees on either side of 90. Ensemble ranges \nwiden for Sunday into early next week, as the ridge looks to build \nfurther east but the timing and location of any subtle waves and \nassociated sfc fronts leads to greater uncertainty at this range.",
